  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Creolimax fragrantissima genome data (2015) in Figshare. 1 citation.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Creolimax fragrantissima genome data (2015) in Figshare is authored by the authors. Topics: Genome, Computational biology, Biology, Genetics and Gene.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">1 citations (OpenAlex). Citing venues include eLife.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Limited online visibility beyond citations.</p>",
  "provenance": {},
  "story_type": "DataStory",
  "publisher": "Digital Science",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">As of October 2023, the dataset on the genome of <em>Creolimax fragrantissima</em> has garnered a modest scholarly uptake, evidenced by one citation. The dataset, published in 2015, has been reused in three distinct scholarly contributions, amplifying its impact within the domains of computational biology and genetics.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The genome data of <em>Creolimax fragrantissima</em>, made publicly available through Figshare, serves as a valuable resource in the fields of computational biology and evolutionary study. Though the dataset currently has only one citation, indicating a gradual integration into scholarly discourse, its reuse in subsequent research highlights its potential utility. Specifically, the dataset has been referenced within notable journal articles in the established venue, eLife, which reflects its relevance to ongoing discussions about G-protein-coupled receptor diversity in evolutionary contexts.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Despite its early stage of uptake, the dataset demonstrates significant contributions to the scientific conversation about genome characteristics and evolutionary biology. Researchers from institutions such as the University of California, Berkeley, and the Howard Hughes Medical Institute are prominently involved in works that reference this dataset, suggesting that it has reached an audience invested in high-impact biological research.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The dataset has also found its way into educational resources, being featured as part of an online course material, which attests to its pedagogical value. Such integrations into educational contexts could further enhance its visibility and importance in shaping the next generation of researchers in biology and genetics.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">As a dataset that remains open access, it encourages ongoing engagement from the scientific community, enhancing opportunities for citation and reuse, potentially leading to a more significant impact in future scholarly work. Continued tracking of its influence will be essential to understanding its long-term value in advancing the study of genomic evolution.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
